# {{.Name}}

A Sora background-worker extension (runtime v2).

The worker script runs periodically in a deliberately small sandbox: it can
append to the extension log and - because this template also requests
`net.fetch` - make allowlisted HTTP requests. No filesystem, no process
control, no raw sockets.

## The worker envelope

The app-side JS runner executes `worker.js` with two globals:

- `worker.log(message)` - appends a line to the extension log (visible in
  the extension details screen).
- `worker.fetch(url, {method, headers, body})` - HTTPS request to a host in
  `network_hosts`. Rejects when the host is not allowlisted.

The manifest declares `interval_minutes` as a wish; the runtime clamps the
effective interval into [15 min, 7 days]. Every run's outcome (ok, failed,
panicked) is written to the extension log.

## Develop / ship

```sh
sora-ext validate
sora-ext pack . -o {{.ID}}.soraext
sora-ext sign --key .sora/author.key {{.ID}}.soraext
```

`network_hosts` entries are lowercase DNS names or `*.example.com`
wildcards - no IPs, schemes, ports or paths. `api.example.com` matches only
itself; `*.example.com` matches subdomains but not the bare domain.
